<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Erotokritos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 17th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Erotokritos is a verse romance in the Cretan dialect of Greek written by Vitsentzos Kornaros in the early 17th century under Venetian rule. Its more than 10,000 rhyming fifteen-syllable lines tell of the love of Erotokritos for the princess Aretousa and his trials to win her. The greatest work of the Cretan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, it remained widely known and recited in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> for centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erotokritos.jpg" alt="Erotokritos" width="320" height="518" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Theophilos Chatzimichael · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erotokritos_and_Arethousa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Vitsentzos Kornaros was common in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, and the poet's identity is the main question on which the others depend. Most scholars identify him with Vitsentzos Kornaros, son of Jacob and brother of the writer Andreas Kornaros, who according to archival sources was born in 1553 and died in 1613 or 1614. The last twelve verses of the poem speak of the poet himself. Kornaros drew on an Italian adaptation of a French romance, and several philologists hold that he used the earlier of the two versions studied, one of 1543.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The story is set in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, but its world belongs to no single period: classical references stand beside elements particular to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, such as a jousting tournament. Erotokritos, son of the king's adviser, loves Aretousa, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>, and serenades her under her window. He wins the king's tournament, is exiled when he asks for her hand, and returns in disguise, his identity concealed by magic, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlachs</a> besiege Athens. He saves the king's life in battle, tests Aretousa's faith, reveals himself, marries her and becomes king of Athens.</p><p>Printed editions divide the poem into five parts, following the five acts of classical drama, and its frequent dialogue gives it a theatrical character; love dominates the first, third and fifth parts and heroic deeds the second and fourth. The language is the Cretan dialect, mostly the idiom of Sitia, with few Italian words. Its rhymed couplets use the rhyming of the traditional mantinades.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The romance circulated widely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> through the 17th century. In 1713 a Cretan who had gathered several manuscripts had it printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>; the only surviving manuscript, an unfinished and illuminated copy of 1710, is a less reliable witness. Stefanos Xanthoudides published the first modern edition in 1915. Its themes of love, friendship, courage and patriotism made it popular throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, where it is still recited to music. It inspired Dionysios Solomos and influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostis-palamas">Kostis Palamas</a>, Kostas Krystallis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-seferis">George Seferis</a>, and it has been translated into English in verse by Theodore Stephanides.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Erotokritos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 16th and early 17th centuries Greek-Italian culture flourished: the Cretan school of icon painting, El Greco and the romance <b>Erotokritos</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/venetian-crete">Venetian Crete</a></li></ul></section>
